{"maxId":254,"entries":{"chapter-20-study-the-role-of-social-workers|Chapter 20 Study: The Role of Social Workers":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Social workers assess social problems, support coping, mediate conflict and obtain resources for patients and families; geriatric expertise is not universal among social workers (p277).":["u3"],"copy|Direct work includes counseling, therapy, education and groups. Support groups can help caregivers understand illness and cope with its effects (p277-278).":["u4"],"copy|Linkage work connects patients with services and addresses lack of information, access barriers, stigma and fears about accepting help (p278).":["u5"],"copy|Case management assesses needs, coordinates delivery and reassesses whether services remain useful as circumstances change (p278-279).":["u6"],"copy|Home care addresses adaptation, caregiving, safety, finances, medication misuse, abuse and end-of-life issues; services vary across agencies (p279).":["u7"],"copy|Community agencies combine direct support, mental health care, education, service development and policy roles (p280).":["u8"],"copy|Nursing homes and assisted living require psychosocial assessment, adjustment support, resident advocacy and work with families and staff (p280).":["u9"],"copy|Hospital discharge planning starts early; assess practical capacity and connect the patient with appropriate community or institutional support (p280-281).":["u10"],"copy|Hospice/palliative social work addresses loss, family communication, psychosocial and spiritual needs, transitions and bereavement (p281).":["u11"],"copy|Explore culture and within-group differences rather than assuming shared needs. Poverty, immigration/language barriers, discrimination and sexual or gender identity can affect access and trust (p281-283).":["u12"],"copy|In the United States, more than 50% of people living with HIV are 50 or older; late testing may leave newly diagnosed older adults facing more immediate challenges (p283).":["u13"],"copy|Abuse may require repeated contacts to detect. Reporting duties and procedures vary locally; assess risk and work with protective services when indicated (p283-284).":["u14"],"copy|Cognitive assessment includes function, living conditions, family dynamics, resources and legal/advance-planning needs; provide stage-appropriate dementia support (p284).":["u15"],"copy|Mental illness and substance misuse can impair independence. Do not automatically attribute symptoms to age, dementia or depression; consider isolation, pain and losses (p285).":["u16"],"from-identifying-a-need-to-getting-help-that-lasts|From identifying a need to getting help that lasts":["u248"],"identify-the-social-problem-that-is-changing-the-clinical-outcome|Identify the social problem that is changing the clinical outcome":["u249"],"direct-service-counseling-education-and-group-work|Direct service: counseling, education and group work":["u250"],"linkage-case-management-and-barriers-to-use|Linkage, case management and barriers to use":["u251"],"how-the-work-changes-by-setting|How the work changes by setting":["u252"],"culture-poverty-and-relationships-that-services-can-overlook|Culture, poverty and relationships that services can overlook":["u253"],"recognizing-possible-harm-without-deciding-too-early-who-caused-it|Recognizing possible harm without deciding too early who caused it":["u254"],"cognitive-impairment-mental-illness-and-substance-use|Cognitive impairment, mental illness and substance use":["u24"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u25"],"copy|A named service may be inaccessible because of transportation, eligibility, stigma or fear of losing control (p278–279).":["u26"],"copy|A warning sign of neglect prompts assessment. It does not by itself identify who caused it (p283–284).":["u27"],"copy|People can share an ethnic background and differ substantially in language, resources, migration history and preferences (p281–282).":["u28"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u29"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u30"],"copy|CHAPTER 20 / HAZZARD":["u18"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 20; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u19"],"copy|What social workers contribute":["u20"],"copy|Match help to the setting":["u21"],"copy|Vulnerability and assessment":["u22"],"copy|A referral succeeds only if the service can be used.":["u36"],"copy|A patient may understand that rehabilitation would help yet lack transportation or fear that accepting a home visit will cost them their independence.":["u37"],"copy|The broker identifies an appropriate resource and helps the patient reach it.":["u38"],"copy|The case manager links several services, monitors what happens and revises the plan when needs or eligibility change (p278–279).":["u39"],"copy|At hospital discharge the relevant question is what the patient and family can manage at home.":["u40"],"copy|A list of available services does not establish caregiver availability or ability to use them.":["u41"],"copy|In hospice the same communication skills serve a different purpose: helping people express divergent wishes, navigate transitions and prepare for bereavement (p280–281).":["u42"],"copy|Look for neglect in the setting as well as on examination.":["u43"],"copy|Pressure wounds, poor nutrition and unsanitary living conditions warrant further assessment.":["u44"],"copy|Financial exploitation may have few physical signs.":["u45"],"copy|Fear of discrimination can also make a person reluctant to disclose relationships or seek services, particularly on entering a residential facility (p282–284).":["u46"],"copy|An older adult repeatedly misses appointments after discharge.":["u47"],"copy|The immediate chart label may be “did not attend,” but that observation gives no explanation.":["u48"],"copy|The person may lack transport, be unable to leave the apartment, misunderstand whether the visit is needed, or fear that a professional who sees their living conditions will recommend placement.":["u49"],"copy|A social worker can explore what the appointment means to the patient, what practical resources exist and which obstacle is actually preventing access.":["u50"],"copy|Each explanation leads to a different intervention, and adding another appointment alone addresses none of them (p277–279).":["u51"],"copy|Relatives, neighbors and other informal helpers are part of the assessment.":["u52"],"copy|Ask who the patient identifies as supportive, what those people already do and whether that support can continue.":["u53"],"copy|The person named as next of kin may live far away or have competing care obligations.":["u54"],"copy|A neighbor who shops regularly may be able to notice a change but be unable to provide personal care.":["u55"],"copy|The social worker can build on what the network already does, mediate competing expectations and connect the patient to formal services where gaps remain (p277–279).":["u56"],"copy|Working frequently with older patients is different from training in gerontological social work.":["u57"],"copy|The chapter reports that 76% of social workers in health care settings work with older patients, while emphasizing that not all have specialist geriatric training.":["u58"],"copy|The relevant expertise includes understanding how disability, cognition, family roles, benefits and institutional transitions affect each other.":["u59"],"copy|When making a referral, describe the question: help with adjustment after bereavement, concern about financial exploitation, or coordination of several home services calls for different skills (p277–278).":["u60"],"copy|Direct service helps the person or family address a problem through interaction with the social worker.":["u61"],"copy|After a diagnosis that threatens independence, counseling may focus on grief, changing identity or conflict about accepting help.":["u62"],"copy|Therapy, when appropriate to the clinician's training and the patient's needs, can address a mental health problem.":["u63"],"copy|Education can make unfamiliar medical processes understandable enough for the patient to participate.":["u64"],"copy|None of these activities requires that the person's difficulty be explained exclusively by a psychiatric diagnosis.":["u65"],"copy|A reasonable emotional response to loss can coexist with practical barriers requiring separate action (p277–278).":["u66"],"copy|Table 20-1 distinguishes a group worker, educator, advocate and case manager, among other roles.":["u67"],"copy|A group can be educational, therapeutic, social or focused on support.":["u68"],"copy|Consider a person caring for a spouse with dementia who has interpreted behavioral changes as deliberate hostility.":["u69"],"copy|Information about the illness addresses one need; learning how other caregivers respond addresses a second; contact with people in a similar situation addresses isolation.":["u70"],"copy|A group alone will not provide respite or nighttime assistance, so assess whether the caregiver also needs a service or a change in the care arrangement (p277–279, p284).":["u71"],"copy|An educator gives usable information: what a service does, how a transition will occur and what choices the patient can make.":["u72"],"copy|An advocate helps preserve the patient's perspective when an organization, family or funding rule points toward a plan the patient does not want.":["u73"],"copy|For example, a recommendation for residential care may reflect an assumption that no home support exists.":["u74"],"copy|The advocate can establish whether a feasible home option has been explored.":["u75"],"copy|Advocacy does not require promising a preferred outcome when the necessary service is unavailable or cannot make the situation safe (p278–279).":["u76"],"copy|Mediation is valuable when family members disagree about the meaning of a recommendation.":["u77"],"copy|One relative may equate seeking help with abandonment, another with protection, while the patient sees it as a threat to privacy.":["u78"],"copy|Elicit what each person is trying to preserve and identify the concrete tasks that have become difficult.":["u79"],"copy|A conversation about who can help with meals and transportation may be more productive than a general argument about whether the patient is “independent.”":["u80"],"copy|Return to the patient's priorities and preserve a way to reassess the arrangement if needs change (p277–279).":["u81"],"copy|Brokerage connects an identified need to a resource.":["u82"],"copy|To make a useful referral, the worker needs to know the service's actual eligibility criteria, cost, geographical reach and practical availability.":["u83"],"copy|The patient may need help making the first contact or understanding what information the agency requests.":["u84"],"copy|Lack of information is only one barrier.":["u85"],"copy|If the transport service stops before the patient's return journey or the service user cannot communicate in its working language, a successful telephone referral has not established access (p278–279).":["u86"],"copy|Case management is necessary when several services depend on one another.":["u87"],"copy|A patient may need a rehabilitation appointment, a means of reaching it, someone to assist with transfers and a mental health contact after a difficult loss.":["u88"],"copy|The case manager assesses needs, arranges and coordinates the services, follows whether they were actually delivered and revises the plan.":["u89"],"copy|Check what happened from the patient's perspective: a home visit that arrived when the person was at therapy may count as an attempted service for the agency but delivered no help (p278–279).":["u90"],"copy|Assess stigma and previous experience before interpreting refusal.":["u91"],"copy|Some people worry that accepting help will expose a mental illness, disclose family conflict or lead inevitably to loss of their home.":["u92"],"copy|Others have experienced discrimination by institutions and have good reasons for caution.":["u93"],"copy|Explain the proposed worker's role and the specific issue that can be addressed, then ask what support the person would find acceptable.":["u94"],"copy|A patient may decline one agency but accept a different arrangement that meets the same need and preserves important relationships or privacy (p278–279, p281–283).":["u95"],"copy|A home visit reveals the environment in which the plan must work.":["u96"],"copy|The social worker may find that stairs, a lack of food, inability to afford medication or an exhausted caregiver is more important to the current problem than the medical team's initial explanation.":["u97"],"copy|Assess adaptation to illness, safety, finances, relationships, substance use and end-of-life concerns as appropriate.":["u98"],"copy|Table 20-2 links these activities to setting.":["u99"],"copy|A home health agency may offer a narrower set of visits than a community organization, so identify which needs can be addressed during the episode and who can remain involved afterward (p279–280).":["u100"],"copy|Community health and mental health agencies may combine counseling and case management with outreach, group programs, education, planning or policy work.":["u101"],"copy|A patient isolated by mobility problems may never appear at a clinic to request help, so community-based contact can change who obtains care.":["u102"],"copy|At the program level, repeated difficulty obtaining transportation for many patients can signal a gap in local services rather than many unrelated individual failures.":["u103"],"copy|A social worker can address the immediate referral and contribute to developing a more accessible service for others (p279–280).":["u104"],"copy|Long-term residential care introduces a different source of distress: an institution has become the person's home.":["u105"],"copy|Ask which routines, relationships and choices matter and what changed at admission.":["u106"],"copy|The social worker can help a resident adapt, advocate when preferences are overlooked, and support families who feel grief or guilt about placement.":["u107"],"copy|Staff may interpret withdrawal as a fixed characteristic unless someone learns that the person stopped participating after losing contact with an important companion.":["u108"],"copy|Work with the facility and the family to bring that information into the care plan (p280).":["u109"],"copy|In the hospital, discharge preparation starts early enough to resolve a practical obstacle before medical readiness.":["u110"],"copy|Determine the previous living situation, what has changed functionally, whether a caregiver is willing and able to help, and which equipment or services can actually be obtained.":["u111"],"copy|If relatives disagree about destination, assess whether the disagreement concerns safety, finances, values or an unrealistic assumption about the help one person can provide.":["u112"],"copy|Placement, home support and rehabilitation are distinct options.":["u113"],"copy|A safe move requires communication with the agency or team receiving the patient (p280–281).":["u114"],"copy|Hospice and palliative settings bring anticipatory loss, family communication, spiritual concerns, practical planning and bereavement to the foreground.":["u115"],"copy|Table 20-3 includes helping patients and families understand options, express preferences and access needed support.":["u116"],"copy|Someone may wish to remain at home while a partner fears being unable to manage symptoms overnight.":["u117"],"copy|Both positions should be heard.":["u118"],"copy|Explore what assistance is available and whether it addresses the specific concern.":["u119"],"copy|A preference for dying at home is meaningful without being sufficient proof that such a plan is safe or sustainable (p281).":["u120"],"copy|Culturally responsive assessment starts by asking, not assigning a preference from the patient's perceived group membership.":["u121"],"copy|Language, migration history, education, family structure, religion and experiences of discrimination may differ even between patients who identify with the same community.":["u122"],"copy|Ask how the person prefers decisions to be discussed and whom they wish to include.":["u123"],"copy|Avoid making a relative responsible for complex communication simply because that person is present.":["u124"],"copy|Consider whether the offered service will be understandable, trusted and acceptable to this individual (p281–282).":["u125"],"copy|Poverty can impose choices invisible in the clinical record.":["u126"],"copy|A person may understand the instructions but be unable to afford transportation, food, housing or medication at the same time.":["u127"],"copy|Financial strain can also affect a family member's ability to reduce paid work to provide care.":["u128"],"copy|Ask about concrete tradeoffs without assuming that failure to follow a plan reflects low motivation.":["u129"],"copy|Determine which benefit, service or advocacy would change the limiting resource, and reassess whether the patient's circumstances improved after the referral (p281–282).":["u130"],"copy|Older immigrants and refugees may face language barriers, unfamiliar services, interrupted support networks or differing expectations of formal care.":["u131"],"copy|Migration experience can include loss or trauma that a routine social history misses.":["u132"],"copy|At the same time, cultural knowledge, family ties and community organizations can be sources of resilience.":["u133"],"copy|Identify the language the person uses most comfortably, who already provides trustworthy support and whether the patient knows how to reach an appropriate service.":["u134"],"copy|Immigration category alone does not establish the person's beliefs, eligibility or preference for family involvement (p282).":["u135"],"copy|Sexual orientation and gender identity can affect disclosure, trust and access, particularly on entering a residential setting.":["u136"],"copy|The person who provides daily care may be a partner or chosen family member whom the service has not recognized.":["u137"],"copy|Ask privately which relationships matter and whom the patient wants included, and avoid disclosing personal information more widely than the patient intends.":["u138"],"copy|A reluctance to discuss an important relationship can reflect prior discrimination.":["u139"],"copy|Respectful inquiry can reveal a strong source of support that a narrowly defined family history would otherwise miss (p282–283).":["u140"],"copy|The chapter notes that more than half of Americans living with HIV are aged 50 or older.":["u141"],"copy|Some have lived with infection and its social consequences for many years, while others are newly diagnosed after delayed testing.":["u142"],"copy|These circumstances can involve different losses, stigma, treatment needs and support networks.":["u143"],"copy|Ask about the meaning of the diagnosis, the availability of help and whether confidentiality concerns are interfering with care.":["u144"],"copy|Do not presume that an older patient has no sexual health concerns or that everyone around the patient already knows the diagnosis (p283).":["u145"],"copy|One way to detect overlapping barriers is to ask who the patient would actually call if the plan failed this evening.":["u146"],"copy|The answer may reveal that the identified caregiver does not speak the service's language, cannot enter the patient's building, or does not know the diagnosis.":["u147"],"copy|Clarify with the patient which information may be shared, what support each person has agreed to provide and how a gap can be filled.":["u148"],"copy|This is especially important at transitions, when several organizations may assume someone else has already established these facts (p278–283).":["u149"],"copy|Table 20-4 distinguishes physical, emotional, sexual and financial abuse from neglect.":["u150"],"copy|A person with pressure wounds, poor hygiene, inadequate intake or unsafe housing requires assessment of needs and safety, but one finding alone rarely identifies its cause.":["u151"],"copy|Disability, limited resources, inadequate caregiving, self-neglect or deliberate mistreatment may produce overlapping signs.":["u152"],"copy|Document the observation and clarify what help was required, who agreed to provide it and what actually occurred.":["u153"],"copy|A discrepancy between accounts warrants further investigation without automatically establishing intent (p283–284).":["u154"],"copy|Financial exploitation may have fewer obvious physical signs.":["u155"],"copy|Ask about sudden difficulty paying for necessities, control of money and whether transactions reflect the person's wishes.":["u156"],"copy|A relative who helps with banking can be a valued support, a source of risk, or both in different respects.":["u157"],"copy|Interview the patient in circumstances that permit an account free of intimidation.":["u158"],"copy|If the person is afraid that disclosure will lead to loss of the only available caregiver, the safety plan must address the dependence as well as the suspected exploitation (p283–284).":["u159"],"copy|The chapter emphasizes that detection can take repeated contacts.":["u160"],"copy|A brief encounter in the presence of a possible perpetrator may not elicit a disclosure.":["u161"],"copy|Use the multidisciplinary team and appropriate protective services, recognizing that reporting duties and procedures vary by jurisdiction.":["u162"],"copy|Immediate safety, the person's ability to participate, the reliability of available support and the risk of retaliation all matter to the response.":["u163"],"copy|Follow up rather than treating the act of making a referral as proof that the person is now protected (p283–284).":["u164"],"copy|When cognition declines, assess function in its usual context.":["u165"],"copy|Who manages meals, appointments and money?":["u166"],"copy|Which errors are new, and what support is already compensating for them?":["u167"],"copy|Education can help family members interpret disease-related behavior, while practical planning addresses safety, caregiver stress and future decision making.":["u168"],"copy|A person may need help with complex arrangements yet still express priorities about daily care, relationships and place of residence.":["u169"],"copy|Tailor support to the current stage and revisit the plan as abilities and caregiver resources change (p284).":["u170"],"copy|Table 20-5 brings together conditions that can complicate assessment, including dementia, depression, anxiety, substance use and longstanding severe mental illness or developmental disability.":["u171"],"copy|The chapter gives a general depression prevalence of about 5%, but rates up to 44% in some homebound and socially isolated populations.":["u172"],"copy|These are estimates for different groups, not two competing answers about a patient's diagnosis.":["u173"],"copy|Depression may present with somatic or cognitive concerns and anxiety as restlessness or trouble concentrating.":["u174"],"copy|Ask about onset, distress and functional change before attributing the presentation to age or dementia (p284–285).":["u175"],"copy|Alcohol, prescribed psychoactive agents and other substances deserve sensitive, specific questioning.":["u176"],"copy|Widowhood, retirement, isolation, pain and disability can change both substance use and its consequences.":["u177"],"copy|Falls or confusion may be the first visible problem.":["u178"],"copy|A person may be using prescribed medicines for a genuine symptom while experiencing harm from a combination, particularly when alcohol or other sedatives are involved.":["u179"],"copy|Communicate the concrete exposure and harm to the medical team and assess the social or emotional issue the person is trying to manage.":["u180"],"copy|This permits a safer plan without dismissing the original symptom (p284–285).":["u181"],"copy|Across these situations, good follow-up tests whether the intervention changed the problem the patient identified.":["u182"],"copy|Does the person now reach appointments, feel safe discussing a relationship, have help preparing meals, or know whom to contact during a caregiving crisis?":["u183"],"copy|An agency may record a completed assessment while the patient remains unable to use a service.":["u184"],"copy|The social worker's role includes noticing that difference, revising the plan with the patient and bringing the result back to the clinical team (p277–281, p284–285).":["u185"],"copy|No past exam questions are mapped to this chapter in the 2020–2026 reference list.":["u186"],"copy|1.":["u187"],"copy|What can a social worker add when clinicians and relatives disagree about discharge?":["u188"],"copy|2.":["u189"],"copy|How does direct counseling differ from brokerage?":["u190"],"copy|3.":["u191"],"copy|What does a group add for someone caring for a relative with dementia?":["u192"],"copy|4.":["u193"],"copy|A patient needs rehabilitation, psychotherapy and transportation.":["u194"],"copy|Which role coordinates the whole plan?":["u195"],"copy|5.":["u196"],"copy|Why follow up after making a referral?":["u197"],"copy|6.":["u198"],"copy|Which concerns become more apparent during a home visit?":["u199"],"copy|7.":["u200"],"copy|How can a community social worker reach people beyond those receiving individual counseling?":["u201"],"copy|8.":["u202"],"copy|What should be established before relying on a family discharge plan?":["u203"],"copy|9.":["u204"],"copy|What can social work contribute after a person enters long-term care?":["u205"],"copy|10.":["u206"],"copy|Which hospice tasks extend beyond locating services?":["u207"],"copy|11.":["u208"],"copy|Why ask an immigrant about language and personal history instead of inferring needs from nationality?":["u209"],"copy|12.":["u210"],"copy|Why might someone entering residential care conceal a long-standing relationship?":["u211"],"copy|13.":["u212"],"copy|Which warning signs might prompt assessment of neglect?":["u213"],"copy|14.":["u214"],"copy|What does a dementia assessment need beyond a cognitive test?":["u215"],"copy|15.":["u216"],"copy|Why explore alcohol or other substance use in a newly isolated older adult?":["u217"],"copy|Answer":["u218","u220","u222","u224","u226","u228","u230","u232","u234","u236","u238","u240","u242","u244","u246"],"copy|Explore the patient's goals and actual supports, facilitate communication and mediate disagreement while advocating for the patient's needs. (p277–278, p280–281)":["u219"],"copy|Counseling helps the person or family cope with a problem. Brokerage connects the person to the service or resource that can meet a need. (p277–278)":["u221"],"copy|Education about the illness, contact with peers facing similar issues and support in coping with its effects. (p277–278)":["u223"],"copy|Case management: assess needs and eligibility, arrange and coordinate services, monitor delivery and reassess as conditions change. (p278–279)":["u225"],"copy|Cost, lack of information, stigma or access barriers may prevent use. Confirm what happened and adjust the arrangement if it failed. (p278–279)":["u227"],"copy|Home conditions, practical safety, caregiver demands, finances, medication use and how the patient adapts to illness in daily life. (p279)":["u229"],"copy|Through community education, consultation, group programs, service development and work on local policy. (p280)":["u231"],"copy|What care is needed, who is available and able to provide it, and what additional services can actually be arranged. (p280–281)":["u233"],"copy|Assess adjustment and psychosocial needs, advocate for the resident and work with staff and family as care needs or relationships change. (p280)":["u235"],"copy|Facilitating family communication, addressing emotional and spiritual concerns, supporting transitions and helping relatives through loss and bereavement. (p281)":["u237"],"copy|Experiences vary with language proficiency, reasons for migration, resources and time spent in the country. Those details change what help is accessible. (p281–282)":["u239"],"copy|Prior discrimination or fear of unequal treatment can make disclosure feel unsafe. Ask whom the person wants involved and protect their privacy. (p282–283)":["u241"],"copy|Poor nutrition or hygiene, pressure wounds, inadequate access to medical care or unsafe living conditions. Explore circumstances, safety and available support. (p283–284)":["u243"],"copy|Information about function at home, the physical and social environment, family relationships, available resources and legal or advance-planning needs. (p284)":["u245"],"copy|Loss, pain or isolation may contribute to misuse. Changes in mood or function can otherwise be misattributed to aging or cognitive illness. (p285)":["u247"]}}
